# ~/.config/labwc/autostart   (Raspberry Pi OS Bookworm and later use labwc)
#
# Install it as your login user, not with sudo:
#
#   mkdir -p ~/.config/labwc
#   cp /etc/xdg/labwc/autostart ~/.config/labwc/autostart   # if the system one exists
#   nano ~/.config/labwc/autostart                          # append the line below
#   chmod 644 ~/.config/labwc/autostart
#
# Permissions: 644 on the file, 755 on ~/.config/labwc, owned by you. labwc
# runs this through `sh`, so it does not need the execute bit.
#
# IMPORTANT: a user autostart REPLACES /etc/xdg/labwc/autostart, it does not
# add to it. On Pi OS the system file is what starts the desktop panel and
# wallpaper, so copy it first unless you want a bare kiosk screen.
#
# Copy it ONCE. Appending the system file a second time gives you two panels,
# two desktops, and two of anything else it launches. If you are redoing this,
# edit the existing file rather than appending to it again.
#
# On a short panel - a 1280x400 bar display especially - consider not starting
# the desktop panel at all: it costs 30-odd pixels of a screen that has none
# to spare, on a machine nobody is going to use as a desktop. A kiosk
# autostart can be this one line and nothing else.
#
# Do NOT set the display mode here. On Raspberry Pi OS the labwc session runs
# kanshi, which owns output configuration and re-applies its own profile on
# every hotplug - anything wlr-randr does from this file gets stamped over.
# See docs/display-modes.md for where display settings actually belong.

/opt/unifi-dashboard/deploy/kiosk.sh &

# Screen blanking is a separate setting, not something to script here:
#   sudo raspi-config  ->  Display Options  ->  Screen Blanking  ->  No
